00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.550 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  we warned you in a past video here on  visualpolitik south korea's new  president the conservative yoon sok yol  is turning out to be a political  earthquake since he took over the  presidency not a week goes by without  yoon announcing some major change in the  country of course given the track record  of south korean presidents when it comes  to keeping their promises we will have  to keep a close eye on what is finally  done and not done  [Music]  after all in the history of south korean  democracy presidents fulfill on average  around 30 percent of their election  pledges because yes in south korea these  things are counted former president moon  for example met just 17 according to  provisional data of course we are  talking about statistics and that  doesn't always work in politics not all  commitments carry the same weight be  there as it may the fact that president  yoon does not come from professional  korean politics may make his statistics  much better for now if there is one  thing we can say it is that the guy  looks like he's got the drive  [Music]  for the time being the vision of south  korea that the new president seems to  want to create is quite different from  the one left by former president moon  now do you want to know exactly what the  first steps are that yoon is taking to  revive south korea could they be the  gateway to a new economic takeoff what  does this president's promise shift to  the right consist of in practice well in  this video we will answer these and  other questions  [Music]  visualpolitik fans south korea's  political system is presidential but  perhaps one of its most notable  characteristics is that legislative and  presidential elections do not coincide  on the same date that is the country's  president may have to govern without a  majority in parliament this is precisely  the scenario that yoon seok yol  encountered specifically between 10th of  may 2022 when he assumed the presidency  and april 2024 he will have no choice  but to govern with a hostile parliament  this means that for at least two of the  five years of his mandate the president  may be tied hand and foot if he does not  get the support of the opposition in the  national assembly all that during this  time he decides to govern more or less  by presidential decree which is exactly  what seems to be going to happen  [Music]  in south korea the legislative chamber  is not required to approve each and  every presidential decree which gives  the president broad powers basically  allowing him to introduce major changes  without the need to reach a large  consensus and although in the long run  these measures can be easily reversed  they can have important consequences on  the political economic and social life  of the country  [Music]  for the time being the first proposals  and the first changes are already here  judge for yourselves  more market and less state  [Music]  as we already mentioned in the last  video about the turnaround in south  korea if there is one thing that  categorizes yoon it is that he does not  like anything absolutely anything that  carries the slightest whiff of left-wing  politics neither socially nor  economically also he says be that as it  may it is in the economic area where  most changes are expected to take place  in the coming years and they are so  important what do you want me to say we  are obliged to tell you about them on  visualpolitik well first of all the new  president has already announced the  central measures of his five-year  economic plan they are already public so  we are not talking about promises or  imaginings but about firm intentions and  among them reducing regulations on  business activity and lowering taxes are  the clear protagonists  regulations and schemes that undermine  business competitiveness and discourage  entrepreneurship will be drastically  improved the current economic  circumstance inside and outside the  country are very serious in times of  crisis we need to make changes to be  more market-orientated and have the  private sector drive the entire economy  yoon syuk yul in fact both finance  minister chu kyung-ho and other  ministers have made it very clear in a  statement that the core of the young  government's economic plan will be  deregulation above all else  [Music]  the new economic plan commits the  government to minimizing state  intervention in the market and to  promoting a change in the economic model  towards one in which private sectors and  companies are the dominant players  likewise the plan also includes reducing  the top corporate tax rate from 25  percent to 22 percent the same level as  it was in 2018 thus reversing the  increases led by moon jai in also and  importantly a revision of the tax system  is proposed to introduce a tax exemption  for profits repatriated to south korea  the aim is to resolve once and for all  the problem of double taxation on the  activities of south korean companies  abroad  and that's not the end of it  yoon naturally also wants more  entrepreneurship and startups to be  created in south korea so in order to  achieve this his idea is to grant  greater tax credits for example by  extending exemptions for stock options a  form of employee compensation that  consists of the delivery of shares in  the company's own stock from the current  threshold of 50 million won the new  government wants to quadruple the tax  exemption to 200 million won about 154  000 in other words if you are  compensated in shares up to that amount  you would not be taxed with this the  government hopes to attract more highly  qualified workers to the startups and  that they will be able to be paid a good  chunk of their salary in shares instead  of cash the company wins the workers win  and the government wins if it increases  the number of startups and don't forget  that we're talking about south korea in  this country people dream of working in  large conglomerates thanks to the high  salaries which allows the large  companies to behave as a kind of talent  vacuum cleaner that damages the  competitiveness of small companies that  are generally underdeveloped we've  already told you about the enormous  problem of duality that south korea has  in this area they are almost two  different countries then another of the  economic measures announced is the  modification of the inheritance tax and  take note because this is not a minor  issue  those of you who have been following us  for a while on visualpolitik may have  already seen this video but for those of  you who haven't i think these headlines  say quite a lot  an inheritance tax bill you can't fathom  10.8 billion dollars the samsung family  in south korea will also donate billions  of dollars worth of art including  picasso and monet  high inheritance tax hampering  entrepreneurship and visualpolitik fans  although south korea has one of the  lowest fiscal pressures in the oecd  dying on the other hand can be very very  expensive provided that is that you have  money because according to the korean  national tax service only 2.9 of the  heirs of deceased persons had to pay  this tax in 2020. but if we're talking  about attacks that in south korea can be  up to 50 percent even between parents  and children following a progressive  scale with deductions in the best income  tax style now do you understand why the  heirs of lee kun hee the former chairman  of samsung group which is the country's  largest business conglomerate have to  face such a hefty bill  [Music]  well although this tax mainly affects  people who have a lot of money whether  you think it is fair or not is up to you  it is true that its impact on the  economy can be considerable you see  since major business conglomerates in  south korea are family owned and  therefore inherited when someone passes  away the bill can be so high that it can  end up affecting the company itself when  it comes to undertaking new investments  or hiring workers for example the heirs  may end up going into debt to pay the  tax by pledging company resources as  collateral and above all this encourages  many people to take money out of the  country and start businesses abroad well  whether you like it or not this is  something that yoon wants to change but  even he doesn't seem to know exactly how  he's going to do it  [Music]  apart from the measure just mentioned  yoon's plan also includes other very  important tax measures such as the  abolition of capital gains tax for all  investors whose profits do not exceed 15  500 per year in return the idea is to  apply a small tax on those holding more  than 7.7 million dollars in shares it  would also allow the postponement of  gift tax payments in the case of  receiving a business from a family  member and tax incentives for  investments in strategic technologies  such as semiconductors will be extended  well this is how yoon wants to make the  south korean economy much more  competitive and possibly able to weather  the crisis that seems to be looming but  wait a minute because the tax missions  are far from the only changes yoon wants  to push through there is much more to  his flagship plan to restore south korea  to its former momentum listen up  the 180 degree turn  visualpolitik fans the change yoon has  in mind is not just about lowering four  taxes and leaving it at that nothing  could be further from the truth the new  president wants to make a u-turn on some  of the most controversial and sensitive  matters such as energy policy and  pensions for example yoon seems ready to  fulfill one of his big promises one of  the star proposals of his campaign to  bet heavily on nuclear energy in fact  the prime minister has already announced  a preview of the plan that seeks to meet  the carbon neutrality target by 2050 but  instead of doing so with renewables as  the main source he wants nuclear power  to be the main player and renewables to  be the backup  union calls for rational energy mix what  he means by rational above all is that  moons form a plan for decarbonization  seems anything but realistic the  previous government's model combines the  progressive closure of nuclear plants  with a wild commitment to renewables the  problem is that in order to carry it out  the weight of renewables had to increase  to between 61 and 71 of the energy mix  something that most experts consider  simply not feasible  on the other hand the new government has  already announced that it will resume  construction of the two nuclear reactors  cancelled by the previous government of  moon jae-in that it will also extend the  useful life of those that are nearing  their limit and that's not all it also  plans to launch the construction of  several new nuclear reactors to increase  the share of nuclear energy in  electricity production from 27.1 to 34  in the medium term while also seek to  introduce small modular reactors or smrs  which are emerging as major players in  the future of nuclear power into the  country the government will continue to  support the deployment of renewable  energies however the ultimate goal is to  eliminate gas and coal-fired power  generation which currently account for  66 percent of south korea's energy mix  by 2050  then on the issue of housing the  economic plan envisages reducing taxes  on landlords so they can reduce the  prices of rent and gions the peculiar  south korean rental system based on a  gigantic rental deposit but without a  monthly fee  a short term risks remain in the lease  market the government seeks to make  preemptive responses by extending tax  and financial benefits and boosting  supplies chu kyung ho finance minister  on this last matter on the supply side  the government has pledged to soon  unveil a plan to build 2.5 million new  homes in the country we shall see but  perhaps the most delicate reform of all  is precisely that of pensions  [Music]  you see south korea is the country with  the most accelerated demographic aging  in the oecd and its current pension  model based on a funded system through a  public pension fund is not expected to  be able to cope with the number of  pensioners in the near future president  yoon himself has clearly stated that in  his opinion the current model is a  burden for future generations therefore  among other measures he wants to  increase the minimum retirement age from  60 to 65 or even eliminate the  retirement age or grant benefits to  companies to rehire retirees what is  known as active retirement  and take note because this is no joke  according to the south korean statistics  office the working age population will  shrink by more than 35 over the next  three decades a phenomenon that  threatens to sink production while  public spending could skyrocket not only  in pensions but also in health and  social services but this topic is so  significant that we will dedicate an  upcoming video specifically to it but  that does not mean that we are  absolutely finished i've already told  you that this new president has come in  with a vengeance  public institution reform is a task that  cannot be delayed any further public  institution evaluations must be  conducted strictly finances must only be  used where they are needed and financial  resources must be spent kindly and  generously on the truly social weak who  desperately need government support yoon  seok seok-ul we will see how that all  turns out for the time being what has  also changed radically has been the  foreign policy now much closer to the  united states and japan and more  belligerence towards china and north  korea which has meant abandoning the  equidistance of the previous moon  government on major foreign policy  issues take a look at this  biden yoon mull more military exercises  amid north korea tensions  south korea's alignment with u.s at odds  with china analysts say as a new south  korean government deepens the nation's  economic and security ties with the  united states experts are warning that  seoul's long-standing relationship with  china is likely to suffer  as soon as possible we have to go back  to the best days of korea and japan  unless we achieve that even though we  are talking about the future of asia i  don't think it's realistic yoon duk min  new south korean ambassador to japan  well i think with all that that we have  told you the question is clear do you  think that if all these measures are  really implemented south korea could  once again experience a new economic  takeoff do you think these measures are  enough to restore the country's  competitiveness at home and abroad leave  us your answers below and now if you  found this video interesting don't  forget to like it and subscribe to  visualpolitik to keep up to date with  all our news once again thank you very  much for being there all the best i'll  see you next time  [Music]

